WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.880
The Fellowship of the Ring goes through a lot&nbsp;
during The Lord of the Rings, but, after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.560
Ring is finally destroyed, each member finally&nbsp;
gets the ending they deserve. From the wisest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.920
of wizards to the humblest of hobbits, these&nbsp;
are the Fellowship's character endings ranked.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.760
For a wizard who always seems to find himself&nbsp;
in the thick of it, Gandalf's story ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.960
surprisingly enough, with more of a whimper than&nbsp;
a bang. By the time of The Lord of the Rings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.280
Gandalf has been pottering around Middle-earth&nbsp;
for around 2,000 years. He gets particularly busy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.520
towards the end of the Third Age, at which point&nbsp;
he helps to drive the Necromancer out of Mirkwood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.720
sets the Quest of Erebor in motion, and&nbsp;
sees the One Ring on its way to Mount Doom.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.800
Once all of the action dies down&nbsp;
and Aragorn becomes king, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.920
Gandalf enters a kind of early retirement. In&nbsp;
the book version of The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.640
he travels with the four hobbit adventurers&nbsp;
back to Bree and then splits off to visit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.560
the mysterious Tom Bombadil. Two years later, he&nbsp;
hooks back up with the hobbits at the Grey Havens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.800
where he boards a ship with Galadriel, Elrond,&nbsp;
Frodo, and Bilbo, and heads into the West.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.960
This is because Valinor, the land across the sea,&nbsp;
was Gandalf's original home, where he'd lived as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.720
the wise spirit Olórin before being incarnated&nbsp;
as a wizard. Presumably upon his return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:29.440
Gandalf shed his elderly body and settled back&nbsp;
into a quiet, comfortable, and well-deserved life.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.836
"I will not say do not weep,&nbsp;
for not all tears are an evil."

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.600
At the end of The Lord of the Rings,&nbsp;
Merry initially lives with Pippin in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.080
his homeland of Buckland, a hobbit&nbsp;
colony on the edge of the Shire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.240
Eventually, he inherits the title of&nbsp;
"master of Buckland" from his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.280
and becomes known as "Meriadoc the&nbsp;
Magnificent." Evidently impressed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.560
the nominal leader of Buckland, Aragorn makes&nbsp;
Merry a counsellor of his northern kingdom.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.760
Merry also plays the part of the historian,&nbsp;
travelling around Middle-Earth, gathering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:12.240
information, and writing down as much as he can.&nbsp;
This includes a manuscript on pipe-weed called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.840
"Herblore of the Shire," a portion of which&nbsp;
can be found in the prologue of The Fellowship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.760
of the Ring. Much of his writing also ends up&nbsp;
finding its way into the Red Book of Westmarch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.760
the manuscript that Bilbo, Frodo, and Sam&nbsp;
are seen working on in The Lord of the Rings.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.720
Merry also remains a rider of Rohan, a&nbsp;
position that he had been granted after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.680
swearing allegiance to Théoden during&nbsp;
the War of the Ring. He stays Éomer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.880
faithful servant-from-afar for decades&nbsp;
until, long after he becomes an old man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.080
he travels with Pippin down to Rohan —&nbsp;
where he witnesses the passing of the king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.440
Merry then continues on to Gondor, where&nbsp;
he spends the remaining years of his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.960
After Aragorn and Pippin pass on, Merry's&nbsp;
body is laid to rest beside his comrades.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.680
During the War of the Ring,&nbsp;
Pippin swears fealty to Denethor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:12.080
and like Merry in Rohan, he retains his status&nbsp;
as a knight of Gondor long after the war ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.120
This makes him one of Aragorn's loyal followers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.800
and the king rewards him for his service by making&nbsp;
him an official counsellor of the North-kingdom.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.760
As far as his life in the Shire goes, Pippin&nbsp;
inherits his father's title of "thain of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.080
Shire" and becomes the leader of&nbsp;
the Tooks. He also gets married,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.960
settles down, and has a son of his&nbsp;
own. While not a historian like Merry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.320
Pippin does begin collating a huge library,&nbsp;
where he collects various manuscripts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.800
later become pivotal in maintaining&nbsp;
the legacy of the War of the Ring.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:51.200
At the ripe old age of 94, Pippin retires from&nbsp;
his role as thain and joins his lifelong friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.320
Merry as they head south to visit Éomer.&nbsp;
From there, the pair head to Gondor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:01.840
where Pippin eventually dies, as well,&nbsp;
bringing an end to a long and happy life.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.040
Though a royal prince of the Woodland Realm&nbsp;
of Mirkwood, an immortal elf, and a member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:16.000
the Fellowship of the Ring, Legolas' backstory is&nbsp;
never fully fleshed out by J.R.R. Tolkien himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.040
When it comes to the elf's later life, though,&nbsp;
there's a bit more information to be found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.320
and like most of the elves of Middle-earth,&nbsp;
it's a sad and slow state of affairs.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.040
Sooner or later, most of the elves&nbsp;
heed the call to return to Valinor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.920
But Legolas doesn't leave straight away. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.640
he and Gimli go on a number of adventures&nbsp;
throughout Middle-Earth. The pair of heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:45.040
visit the Glittering Caves Aglarond at Helm's&nbsp;
Deep, as well as the ents in Fangorn Forest.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.360
Not one to sit still for long, soon&nbsp;
after arriving back in Mirkwood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.520
Legolas gathers up some of his&nbsp;
people and heads back to Gondor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.960
There, he settles in the garden-like land of&nbsp;
Ithilien, which becomes the fairest country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:05.680
in all the westlands. Even here, though, Legolas&nbsp;
is unable to find peace, and after Aragorn dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:11.280
he builds a ship and sets sail for the Blessed&nbsp;
Realm — with none other than Gimli by his side.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:18.320
Gimli is a sturdy young dwarf during the War&nbsp;
of the Ring, coming in at a youthful 140 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.280
old. This gives him plenty of life to live&nbsp;
after the war, and he puts it to good use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.000
First, he embarks with Legolas on their&nbsp;
trip through Fangorn Forest and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.040
Glittering Caves — and it's in that second&nbsp;
place that Gimli finds his true calling.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.520
At one point during the Battle of Helm's&nbsp;
Deep, Gimli was forced into the caves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.040
and he found himself smitten by their jaw-dropping&nbsp;
beauty. His description of them is so compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.400
that it's precisely what convinces Legolas to&nbsp;
suggest that they visit the caves themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.280
Gimli is so taken with their natural beauty&nbsp;
that he eventually returns there with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:59.040
dwarves and sets up a colony, becoming the&nbsp;
"lord of the Glittering Caves" in the process.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.080
After Aragorn dies, it's recorded that Gimli&nbsp;
is actually granted permission to travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:10.400
with Legolas to the Undying Lands. Seeing the&nbsp;
opportunity to meet the Lady Galadriel again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.040
he accepts, and becomes the only&nbsp;
dwarf to ever journey into the west.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.160
By a great measure, Frodo Baggins suffered the&nbsp;
most out of all the members of the Fellowship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.920
While the others took on armies of orcs, trolls,&nbsp;
and Ringwraiths, Frodo endured a devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:34.320
battle with the One Ring itself. The Ring is&nbsp;
imbued with the power of the Dark Lord Sauron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.880
and Frodo's key victory is the simple&nbsp;
fact that he's actually able to get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.320
Mount Doom in the first place — without having&nbsp;
claimed the Ring for himself along the way.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.960
Unfortunately, Frodo's scars endure&nbsp;
long after the Ring is destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.800
The wounds he endured by the Witch-King's blade&nbsp;
and Shelob's stinger trouble him for many years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.600
with Frodo falling deathly ill on&nbsp;
every anniversary of each stabbing.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:06.160
"It's been four years to the day since&nbsp;
Weathertop, Sam. It's never really healed."

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.640
After the War of the Ring, Frodo spends some time&nbsp;
writing down his story, and then, plagued by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.400
memories of these old wounds, sets sail for the&nbsp;
Undying Lands — in search of a place to finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:24.400
receive the healing and rest he needs. Whether&nbsp;
he dies soon after getting there or lives a long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:30.440
happy life in the immortal country, Frodo finally&nbsp;
finds peace at the end of his long journey.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.440
Boromir is the heir of the steward of Gondor and&nbsp;
one of the finest warriors in the Fellowship. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:43.760
arrives in Rivendell initially seeking answers to&nbsp;
a prophetic dream, attends the Council of Elrond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.880
and before he knows it, finds himself joining&nbsp;
the ranks of the Fellowship of the Ring.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.880
Throughout their journey, Boromir&nbsp;
serves as a crucial protector for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.480
Fellowship — particularly during their battle&nbsp;
in the Mines of Moria. After that, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.400
things begin to turn, and Boromir soon gives&nbsp;
in to the lure of the One Ring. Eventually, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:11.040
tries to take it from Frodo, who narrowly escapes&nbsp;
and heads off toward Mordor with Sam by his side.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.440
While Boromir's actions technically&nbsp;
lead to the breaking of the Fellowship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:21.360
the hero ultimately gets a redemptive ending&nbsp;
— one that is nothing less than truly heroic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.640
He quickly repents his misdeeds and arrives&nbsp;
back on the scene in time to find Merry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:32.880
Pippin being carried off by a pack of pursuing&nbsp;
uruk-hai. Boromir defends the hobbits bravely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.680
but is nonetheless overwhelmed and&nbsp;
fatally wounded by the retreating orcs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:43.120
In the end, the son of Gondor dies in the&nbsp;
company of Aragorn, his friend and king.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.280
"Fly, you fools."

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.800
Since he appears in two separate physical&nbsp;
forms over the course of The Lord of the Rings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:54.400
it's probably worth bringing up&nbsp;
Gandalf again — especially since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:00.240
at the end of his life as Gandalf the Grey, the&nbsp;
Fellowship's wizard literally goes down in flames.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.480
After millennia spent orchestrating the resistance&nbsp;
to Sauron, Gandalf the Grey finds himself swept up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.480
in the events of the War of the Ring.&nbsp;
Never one to shrink from a challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.840
he identifies the One Ring, sends Frodo&nbsp;
on his way, uncovers Saruman's treachery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.280
and then becomes, hands down, the most&nbsp;
powerful member of the Fellowship of the Ring.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:27.360
As Gandalf leads the company south towards&nbsp;
Mordor, they eventually enter Moria. Soon enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:34.160
the Fellowship is attacked by goblins, trolls,&nbsp;
and, of course, a Balrog. Gandalf makes his stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:39.920
against the Balrog on the Bridge of Khazad-dûm,&nbsp;
and, after a brief battle, plunges along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:45.520
it into the depths below. The pair fall to the&nbsp;
deepest, darkest pits of the mountains, then climb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:51.840
up the Endless Stair, and there, at the mountain's&nbsp;
peak, Gandalf finally defeats his enemy. The Grey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:57.960
Wizard then succumbs to his wounds, before being&nbsp;
sent back to Middle-Earth as Gandalf the White.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.880
Of all the characters in The Lord of the Rings,&nbsp;
Samwise Gamgee is the one who finds true peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:10.720
after the end of the war. After he bids&nbsp;
his master farewell at the Grey Havens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:16.720
Sam returns home and inherits Bag End, where he&nbsp;
settles down with his wife, Rosie, with whom he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:23.920
has 13 children. Among these children are four&nbsp;
sons, named Merry, Pippin, Frodo, and Bilbo.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:29.840
Sam is also elected as the mayor of the&nbsp;
Shire for seven consecutive seven-year terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.920
Aragorn subsequently makes Sam a&nbsp;
counsellor of the North-kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:39.600
along with Merry and Pippin, and his daughter&nbsp;
Elanor becomes Queen Arwen's maid of honor.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.360
After over 60 years of&nbsp;
marriage, Rosie passes away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.600
and much like Bilbo long before, Samwise decides&nbsp;
that the time has come for one last journey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.880
He leaves Bag End and heads to the Grey&nbsp;
Havens, where he becomes the last of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.840
Ring-bearers to board a ship and head&nbsp;
over the sea to the Undying Lands.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:06.320
After the War of the Ring, Aragorn becomes&nbsp;
the king of the reunited kingdoms of Gondor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:13.760
and Arnor. Aragorn rules for an impressive 120&nbsp;
years, at which point his son inherits the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:18.240
But Aragorn doesn't die in order for this&nbsp;
to happen — not in a traditional sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.320
at least. In the appendix of The Return&nbsp;
of the King, it's explained that:

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:28.160
"He felt the approach of old age and knew that&nbsp;
the span of his life-days was drawing to an end."

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.200
Bequeathing his crown and scepter to his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:37.120
Aragorn goes to a prepared place of rest and&nbsp;
lies down to surrender his life willingly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:42.880
A distraught Arwen protests, but Aragorn&nbsp;
insists that his time has come. He adds:

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:46.960
"Take counsel with yourself, beloved,&nbsp;
and ask whether you would indeed have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.840
me wait until I wither and rail from&nbsp;
my high seat unmanned and witless."

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.880
Though heartbroken, Arwen lets her&nbsp;
husband go. Aragorn's final words are:

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:01.920
"In sorrow we must go, but not in&nbsp;
despair. Behold! We are not bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.880
for ever to the circles of the world, and&nbsp;
beyond them is more than memory. Farewell!"

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:12.560
And with that, the long life of Gondor's&nbsp;
greatest king finally comes to an end.

